            <fr:title text="Category">Category</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <html:em>category</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}]]></fr:tex> consists of
<html:ol><html:li>A type of objects <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C}_{0}]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>For each pair of objects <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x , y : \mathcal {C}_{0}]]></fr:tex> a <html:em>set</html:em> of
  morphisms <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {C} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex>. We may simply write a moprhism with
  an arrow, denote <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : \mathcal {C} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex>
  as <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : x \to  y]]></fr:tex> or <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x \overset {f}{\to } y]]></fr:tex> or similar
  </html:li>
  <html:li>A composition operation on morphisms. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : x \to  y]]></fr:tex> and
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g : y \to  z]]></fr:tex>, there is a morphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f \star  g : x \to  z]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>For each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x : \mathcal {c}_{0}]]></fr:tex>, an identity morphism
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {id}_{x} : x \to  x]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>Left-unitality of composition: for all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : x \to  y]]></fr:tex>, an equality
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {idL}_{f} : \mathsf {id}_{x} \star  f = f]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>Right-unitality of composition: for all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : x \to  y]]></fr:tex>, an equality
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {idR}_{f} : f \star  \mathsf {id}_{y} = f]]></fr:tex></html:li>
  <html:li>Associativity of composition: for all
  <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : x \to  y]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g : y \to  z]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[h : z \to  w]]></fr:tex>, an equality
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {assoc}_{f , g , h} : {\mathopen {}\left (f \star  {\mathopen {}\left (g \star  h\right )\mathclose {}}\right )\mathclose {}} = {\mathopen {}\left ({\mathopen {}\left (f \star  g\right )\mathclose {}} \star  h\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex></html:li></html:ol></html:p>
            <html:p>Concretely, the
definition above is meant to model <fr:link href="https://github.com/agda/cubical/blob/master/Cubical/Categories/Category/Base.agda" type="external">the one used in the Cubical
standard libary</fr:link>
<fr:link href="/cubicalagdalib/" title="The Cubical Agda Library" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/cubicalagdalib/" display-uri="cubicalagdalib" type="local">[cubicalagdalib]</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>However, the notion of category is
flexible. Depending on the context, we may be talking of small
, locally small, wild, or any other kind of category that may augment
which things we require to be (homotopy) sets, which things we require
to be small types, etc. For the most part, the same idea of a category
will apply across all of these settings.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
